= Starter Sponge =

> Tesco sell fresh yeast in multiples of 100 g.

In a small bowl combine the @lukewarm water{100%ml}, @egg{1}(beaten), @fresh yeast{20%g}(or 7 g dried yeast), @plain flour{55%g}, and @caster sugar{1%tsp}. Cover and leave in a warm draught-free place for about half an hour (or longer if it is not puffy by then).

= Dough =

Place the @mixed dried fruit{255%g}(such as sultanas, raisins, and chopped mixed peel; avoid currants; try dried apricots, unsweetened cranberries, figs, or tart morello cherries) in another small bowl and cover with the boiling @black tea (earl gray preferred){1%cup}. Leave for ~{20%minutes}.

Mix the dry ingredients for the dough (@plain flour{455%g}, @ground mixed spice{40%g}, @ground cardammon{1%tsp}, @caster sugar{85%g}, @salt{1%tsp}, and @baking powder{1%tsp}) in a large bowl. Add the @milk{120%ml} and @butter{85%g}(melted) to the dry dough ingredients. Mix until it begins to come together, then add in the starter sponge and mix well. The dough will be incredibly sticky at this point. Drain the fruit well and sit aside.

Liberally flour the worktop and knead the dough until it is soft and springy. It will take in about quarter to third of a cup of flour during kneading. Flatten the dough into a rough rectangle, sprinkle the fruit over the top, and knead it in briefly, until evenly distributed. Once soft enough to handle, place it in a clean greased bowl and cover with clingfilm. Place it in a warm draught-free spot and allow it to rise for about ~{1%hour} until it has doubled in size.

Knead the dough and return it to the bowl for a further ~{30%minutes}.

Once the dough has risen again, knock it back just enough to work with it and divide the dough into 12 pieces. Roll the pieces into balls and spread them out in a #greased baking tray with deep sides{}. Set aside for ~{30%minutes} in the warm draught-free spot. Heat the oven to 200°C.

Bake for 20 to ~{25%minutes}. If the buns are browning too quickly then cover with foil and reduce the heat to 180°C.